WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.820
[Carl Ide:] In the public interest, the Westinghouse Broadcasting Company

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:07.720
and the University of Pittsburgh, one of the
nation's major health centers,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.530
in cooperation with the American Medical Society and the United States Public Health Service,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:18.980
bring you "The Silent Invader," an up-to-the-minute
report on Asian influenza.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.680
[Dr. Carl Ide:] How do you do? I'm Carl Ide.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.930
Throughout history, mankind has been forced
to wage many battles in order to protect

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:39.690
his family or nation against misfortune, disaster,
or even possible extinction.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:46.120
Now sometimes these wars have been...well
they've been catastrophic.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.070
There was the catastrophe of 1918 when an
influenza epidemic

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.460
swept through our own nation as well as the
entire world.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.620
Unlike the battles of nation-against-nation,
or humanity against the forces of nature,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.900
when man has often had an opportunity to prepare himself,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.660
the battles against disease throughout the
centuries have often found man

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:12.140
in the unfortunate position of having to combat this
enemy only after it had infiltrated

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.610
his community and infected much of the population.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.580
Recently, you've become aware of a pandemic
or a worldwide epidemic,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.250
which originated in the Far East and is now
known as Asian influenza.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.530
Because of the alertness and efficiency of
the United States Public Health Service,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:37.780
and the World Health Organization, a detection
system similar to that of the aircraft spotting

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.640
has been established throughout the world
and has enabled us to recognize

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.980
and follow the progress of Asian influenza as it circles the globe.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:51.790
Health authorities expect an outbreak this
fall and winter in the United States.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:56.080
But not in such portions as in the 1918 epidemic.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.840
Now the man upon whose shoulders rests the
responsibility for preparing the United States

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.080
for the impending battle with Asian influenza, is Dr. Leroy E. Burney,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.030
Surgeon General of the United States Public
Health Service.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:14.460
Dr. Burney, I imagine you and your staff have been gathering

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.220
quite a tremendous amount of material on Asian flu.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.569
Now it seems to me that everybody I know has
had and has talked about influenza.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:25.320
But I don't think anyone really knows what
it is.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.950
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, Carl it's a,
one of the upper respiratory infections,

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.900
something like a cold, only it's caused by
a flu virus.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.950
And the difference between the Asian influenza
and the influenza that we've been having

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:45.310
all of these past few years is that this strain began in, the strain of the virus

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:50.540
began in Hong Kong and spread from Hong Kong to the rest of the world including the United States.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:51.910
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:55.690
Well, every year influenza is a problem in
our communities.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.820
I'm sure it is in yours, too.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.950
Why are we suddenly so all concerned about
it now?

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.849
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, that's a very
good question.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:08.950
We are concerned because in the other countries
in which it's appeared, and even in the outbreaks

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:14.660
that we've had so far in this country, the
attack rate has been 15 to 20 percent of the population.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.430
And this attack rate occurs in about four
to six weeks.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:25.160
For example, in metropolitan Pittsburgh, with
approximately one million people,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:31.150
if we had this flu strike here, you would
have approximately 200,000 people

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.220
who would become ill in a four-to-six weeks' period.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:40.599
And this, of course, would make a tremendous impact upon the economy of this particular area.

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:42.810
[Carl Ide:] That's understandable.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.580
Well, we've all heard of outbreaks here in
this country Dr. Burney, uh, exactly how far has it spread?

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:53.239
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, it's spread all
over the United States.

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:58.599
The first cases occurred, surprisingly enough, on the East Coast rather than on the West Coast,

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:03.129
among Navy personnel at Newport, Rhode Island.

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:05.689
And then the second group occurred a few days later

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.940
in both Navy and civilian personnel in San Diego.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:15.580
Since that time, of course, which was about
the first of June, it has spread in localized outbreaks

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.859
through Grinnell, Iowa, to the Boy Scout
Jamboree in Pennsylvania, to Louisiana,

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:23.669
and to actually almost all of the states in
the United States.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:29.749
[Carl Ide:] I didn't realize that actually
that was Asian flu that hit that Jamboree in our own state. It was Asian flu?

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.560
Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] That's right, it was.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.550
[Carl Ide:] Well, from what I've been able
to learn, Dr. Burney, not many people

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:34.809
have died as a result

00:04:34.809 --> 00:04:37.289
of Asian influenza in the rest of the world.

00:04:37.289 --> 00:04:40.159
Now do you think the same will hold true here
in the United States?

00:04:40.159 --> 00:04:47.009
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] We have the belief,
now based upon the experience

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:49.349
of Asian influenza in the other countries,

00:04:49.349 --> 00:04:54.430
in South America at the present time, where
it is in epidemic proportions,

00:04:54.430 --> 00:05:00.979
and as a result of the outbreaks in this country
so far, that it is a mild infection,

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:04.340
and with a very low mortality rate.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:10.160
And we believe that it will continue to be
both a mild infection and have a very low death rate.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:14.020
[Carl Ide:] Well, is there the possibility
that Asian flu might turn into,

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.500
if I may use the word, a killer?

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:20.559
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, that is a possibility, of course,

00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:24.800
and that has happened in preceding years when we have had epidemics.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:33.249
Back in 1888, when we had a large epidemic, and then many of us recall the 1918-19 epidemic,

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:40.349
when we had a mild influenza epidemic
in the spring of 1918.

00:05:40.349 --> 00:05:46.669
And then in the fall we had a large outbreak
of very virulent influenza,

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:49.380
followed by serious complications.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:54.059
And then another wave hit us in the February
or March of 1919.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:06:00.460
At that time, as you may recall, in that
whole period, we had about 20 million cases

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.140
in the United States, and about 850,000 deaths.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.319
But I would like to add that conditions are
quite different now than they were then.

00:06:09.319 --> 00:06:13.949
In the first place, we have a mild strain
of influenza, not a virulent strain.

00:06:13.949 --> 00:06:20.789
In the second place, we have the antibiotics
to combat any complications that might arise,

00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:22.800
which we did not have in 1918.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.330
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.589
Well doctor, with the disease spreading so
rapidly,

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:30.139
what exactly now are we doing to combat it?

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:35.289
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well actually Carl,
we are doing, trying to do three things.

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:40.879
One is to develop as rapidly as possible the
only preventive agent that we have

00:06:40.879 --> 00:06:46.319
against influenza, and that is the vaccine against this particular strain of the vaccine, of the virus...

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:54.289
and that is being done in six of the large
pharmaceutical manufacturers of this country.

00:06:54.289 --> 00:07:00.409
The strain was collected by an alert U.S. Army medical team in Tokyo,

00:07:00.409 --> 00:07:05.429
and sent back to this country, and then given to the U.S. Public Health Service.

00:07:05.429 --> 00:07:11.929
We distribute it to the manufacturers, and
they, in turn, actually began to manufacture the vaccine

00:07:12.059 --> 00:07:15.619
before we had had the first case in this country.

00:07:15.619 --> 00:07:21.840
So now our job is to get more of that vaccine
available, and to get the public to use it.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.749
Number two, we have to be prepared to take
care of the individuals who become ill.

00:07:27.749 --> 00:07:32.939
And we have been working since early June
with the American Medical Association,

00:07:32.939 --> 00:07:37.069
the American Hospital Association, state and
territorial health officers, voluntary groups,

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:38.069
and others,

00:07:38.069 --> 00:07:41.630
to prepare for taking care of those who become
ill.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:46.979
And finally, the matter of having an alert
and an informed public,

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:50.960
one who will be aware of the facts without
being unduly alarmed.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:57.129
And certainly this program of the Westinghouse
Broadcast Company is a good example

00:07:57.129 --> 00:08:01.209
of public service to have an alert, but not an alarmed public.

00:08:01.209 --> 00:08:07.740
[Carl Ide:] Well Dr. Burney, you and your
public health service crew, if I can use that word, crew,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.649
have certainly seemed to make great strides in order to

00:08:10.649 --> 00:08:14.319
get us ready for this possible epidemic in
such a very short time.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.399
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, we've had a lot
of help, I can assure you Carl, in doing this.

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:22.759
It hasn't been a one-man or a one-organization
show.

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:27.419
As I said previously, we have had the full
cooperation and support of the

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.439
American Medical Association

00:08:29.439 --> 00:08:33.830
in both the educational, as well as the medical
care part.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:39.640
And then in working with the manufacturers to get them to manufacture the vaccine

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.320
and to stop other activities and to build up their staffs.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:48.280
I think we can all be proud of the industry
which we have in this country.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:53.830
It's another example of the know-how and the
expeditious way in which free enterprise,

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:55.970
through our American industry, can do a good
job.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.700
The vaccine, or the virus, was given to the
industries about the middle of May,

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.860
and within three months, vaccine was being released through our National Institutes of Health,

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.580
as being both a safe and an effective vaccine.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:14.060
And that seems to me a good example of both teamwork

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.250
and a very expeditious work by the manufacturers.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:18.480
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.550
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Another example of
this teamwork, and to illustrate that this

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:31.240
isn't a one-man show is that we have with us this evening, I'd like to present to you, Dr. James A. Crabtree,

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:36.580
who's Associate Dean of the University of
Pittsburgh Graduate School of Public Health

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:41.390
who will discuss how Asian influenza has spread
throughout the world.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:45.220
[Carl Ide:] Well, thank you very much, Dr.
Burney, and I'm awfully glad

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.000
that Dr. Crabtree is here with us tonight, too.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.100
How do you do, Dr. Crabtree?

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:54.340
We've been discussing the fact that this Asian flu has covered a lot of territory in a short time

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.160
But I would like to know how did it all get started?

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:04.460
[Dr. James A. Crabtree:] Well, the exact point
of origin of the influenza virus

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:05.930
is of course not known.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:14.670
But the current epidemic had its beginning,
as Dr. Burney indicated, in Hong Kong and Singapore.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:19.870
Uhh, this was in early April.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:26.130
By mid-April the disease had become indeed
epidemic in both of these cities

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:30.940
and remained so throughout the rest of the
month.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:34.670
From Hong Kong and Singapore, the disease
rapidly, in early May,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:39.760
moved to the island of Formosa.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:44.160
And here, it's estimated that as many as a
half million people

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.030
were attacked within a relatively short time.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:51.570
From Formosa, also during the month of May,

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:58.360
the disease next made its appearance in Borneo and Japan.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:05.290
And from the major shipping centers, from
these several areas, the disease very rapidly

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:15.300
scattered into widely, such widely separated places as Malaya, Indochina, Indonesia,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:21.480
India, Guam, and the Philippines.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:28.620
The first cases...also in late May, the disease
was reported as far away as Australia,

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:36.790
and on board several ships en route from Australia to San Francisco.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:42.160
The first appearance of the disease in the
United States was on June 2nd.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:49.900
This date is important because ahh, it means
only a short six weeks from the time

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:55.060
the first cases were reported in China, they occurred in the United States.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:12:00.130
This indicates the great rapidity with which
influenza and influenza-like diseases

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:04.130
can spread over the world.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:12.540
By mid-June, this disease had practically
circled the globe, including southeast Asia,

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.830
southwest Asia, the Middle East,

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:20.040
Europe, Australia, and North America.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:24.780
Late in June, the disease made its appearance
in North Africa, introduced probably

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:30.940
by the Moslems returning from their pilgrimage, pilgrimages to Mecca.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:37.610
So that finally in late July, in early July,
the disease made its appearance in Chile,

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:39.010
in South America.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:46.920
So that in three short months, every continent
on the globe had been involved

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:47.950
in this great pandemic.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:52.540
[Carl Ide:] Well Dr. Crabtree, you mentioned
that it had been reported on ships en route

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:56.660
to San Francisco, and that several cases were definitely diagnosed,

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:58.490
among our fleet at Newport, Rhode Island.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:02.870
Now wouldn't it have been comparatively simple to have isolated these known cases

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:06.440
and prevented the spreading of them, and kept them from coming in at all?

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.430
[Dr. James A. Crabtree:] Unfortunately, not
so simple.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:16.790
For the reason that quarantine against influenza
is not effective.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:23.490
Had it been possible for all of the cases
on board these ships to have been isolated,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:29.980
it would have had little effect on the actual
spread of the disease because undoubtedly there were

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:35.990
many more people on board these same ships, who by reason of a more recently acquired infection,

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:41.360
would have shown no symptoms whatever
of the disease, and therefore,

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:45.810
they could not have been readily identified if they were passing through a quarantine station.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:46.830
[Carl Ide:] I see.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.170
Well, Dr. Burney had mentioned that we had
approximately

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:53.500
25,000 known cases in this nation.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.180
Are they all bunched in one area of the United
States?

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:57.820
Or has the virus spread all over?

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.860
[Dr. James A. Crabtree:] The virus has spread
all over.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:06.650
We have some maps here in the studio that
the television audience can see,

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:12.670
which graphically shows how the disease has
spread in the United States,

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:18.960
starting from Newport, Rhode Island and the West Coast.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:24.160
During the month of June, the disease had
spread to Salt Lake City, Utah;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.940
to Grinnell, Iowa;

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:35.520
Cleveland, Ohio; and a few cases along the
East Coast.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:43.040
If we could have the map from showing the
spread in July, we see that the disease,

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:51.020
starting from these original centers, has literally moved in practically every direction throughout the country.

00:14:51.029 --> 00:14:58.720
Then the final map sort of speaks for itself
in that it indicates that this virus has become

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:06.650
very well-seeded in practically every state
in the country, certainly in every major region

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:07.650
of the country.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:12.660
[Carl Ide:] Well, I think I've got a pretty
good idea of how influenza spreads, Dr. Crabtree.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.880
But I'm still wondering about the virus itself.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.620
Uh, what kind of an animal is it?

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:24.051
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well Carl, I'd like to present to you one of our staff from the National Institutes of Health

00:15:24.051 --> 00:15:27.940
of the Public Health Service, Dr.
Dorland Davis, who is the Associate Director

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:33.720
of our National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, and ask him to answer this question

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.260
and any other scientific questions that you
might have.

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:37.260
[Carl Ide:] Well fine.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:38.260
Thank you again, Dr. Burney.

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.110
Dr. Davis, it's awfully nice to have you

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.040
take your time and come see us tonight.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:44.770
I'm going to ask what is probably going to
seem like a silly question.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.420
But I'd like to know what is this virus like?

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:48.420
What is this virus?

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:53.589
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Well, to begin with Carl,
influenza is a specific disease

00:15:53.589 --> 00:15:56.310
caused by a specific virus.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:02.700
This virus was first isolated back in 1931
by Dr. Shope at the Rockefeller Institute.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:09.029
And then subsequently in 1933, it was shown
to cause human disease by British scientists.

00:16:09.029 --> 00:16:10.029
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:16:10.029 --> 00:16:16.269
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Now we know that there are three major classifications of virus.

00:16:16.269 --> 00:16:18.780
Types A, B, and C.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:25.780
And more recently a new type, a fourth type,
D, has been described in Japan.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:26.940
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:28.020
Mm-hm.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:31.480
Well this A, B, C, and D, is this fourth type,
the D Type,

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:32.790
is that the Asian flu?

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:33.790
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] No. No.

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:35.440
[Carl Ide:] You said something about it being
related to Type A.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.020
That's what confuses me, the A, B,and C [chuckles].

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:39.140
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] No.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.290
The Type A influenza is the influenza

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:48.660
which commonly causes epidemics and pandemics
at intervals throughout the recent years.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:49.660
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:50.200
Mm-hm.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.780
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Now the Asian-type of
virus is a Type A, and it causes the kind

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.680
of disease which we have had in recent years.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:16:59.810
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:07.009
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] So, now the, however,
the antigenic composition of this Asian type

00:17:07.009 --> 00:17:14.620
is different from that which we have described
in the more recent strains.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:23.179
So that a person who is immune to the older strains is probably not immune to this new strain.

00:17:24.199 --> 00:17:30.190
And thus the vaccines which have been developed
in the recent years by Drs Francis, Salk,

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:31.669
and others,

00:17:31.669 --> 00:17:35.879
have no, probably no effect on this invader from Asia.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.899
[Carl Ide:] Well, I guess this is one of the
reasons I'd never get into medical school.

00:17:39.899 --> 00:17:40.899
[Chuckles]

00:17:40.899 --> 00:17:44.710
So a new vaccine has to be made each time a new strain comes along.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:45.710
That is correct, is it?

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:47.290
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Yes.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.060
[Carl Ide:] I mean that explains why we don't have this vaccine in stock, the live vaccine in stock.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:52.140
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Yes.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.460
That is right.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:56.419
[Carl Ide:] Well, I think everybody's hearing
a good deal about the Asian flu story,

00:17:56.419 --> 00:17:59.759
but will a person know if he has Asian flu?

00:17:59.759 --> 00:18:00.869
If so, how?

00:18:00.869 --> 00:18:04.440
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Well, I think he will know.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:11.180
Sometimes his physician finds it a little
difficult to diagnosis it because the symptoms, may be,

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:17.429
are similar to those caused by other respiratory viruses or actually, respiratory bacteria.

00:18:17.429 --> 00:18:23.159
So that, to be absolutely certain, we must
use laboratory methods

00:18:23.159 --> 00:18:25.110
to make a specific diagnosis.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:28.210
Now these are generally of two kinds.

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.029
One is the actual isolation of the virus.

00:18:31.029 --> 00:18:36.909
Or the second is the demonstration of antibodies
which appear in the blood of the person who,

00:18:36.909 --> 00:18:38.390
while he has the disease.

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:40.789
Now this often takes a little time,

00:18:40.789 --> 00:18:44.809
and it really doesn't help the patient's position very much,

00:18:44.809 --> 00:18:49.990
but it is important for us to know what kind
of disease is prevalent in the community,

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:58.280
and to discover what the type is and follow its course.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:18:58.780
[Carl Ide:] I see.

00:18:58.789 --> 00:19:03.210
Well naturally, the thing most people are
concerned about is the vaccine, first of all,

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:06.259
and particularly how it works in the system.

00:19:06.259 --> 00:19:11.940
Now it seems to me that, being made from real
Asian influenza virus,

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.950
wouldn't there be some danger in being vaccinated?

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:14.950
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] No.

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:15.950
There's no danger at all.

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:20.200
The vaccine is completely killed during the
process of being prepared.

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:21.200
[Carl Ide:] Mm-hm.

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:25.640
[Dr. Dorland Davis:] Now the vaccine is made by injecting this virus

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:30.000
into fertile chicken eggs where the virus grows.

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:35.970
And then by processing the fluids, the vaccine
is made by the manufacturers.

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:40.980
It requires about forty days to manufacture and to test this vaccine.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:42.559
[Carl Ide:] I see.

00:19:42.559 --> 00:19:47.459
Well Dr. Burney, I can breathe a lot easier
knowing a little about how this vaccine is made

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:52.340
and that there isn't any live virus in it, and I'm certainly impressed with the way

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:54.429
your public health team operates.

00:19:54.429 --> 00:19:55.429
I mentioned it before,

00:19:55.429 --> 00:19:57.999
I want to impress that on you for our viewers
as well as myself.

00:19:57.999 --> 00:19:58.999
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Thank you.

00:19:58.999 --> 00:20:02.059
[Carl Ide:] Up to now, I think that you've
given me and the rest of the audience

00:20:02.059 --> 00:20:05.220
a complete picture of the Asian flu.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:09.710
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] Well, I rather doubt,
Carl, that we've given you a complete picture,

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:14.570
because we've left out one of the most important
groups of our total team

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:17.600
in meeting the challenge of this new Asian
influenza,

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:21.499
and that is the American Medical Association,
which represents

00:20:21.499 --> 00:20:25.510
the physicians throughout our entire country.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.070
We have worked with them from the beginning
of this and have had complete support

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:31.119
and understanding from that group.

00:20:31.119 --> 00:20:37.179
And I would like to present to you here now,
the Secretary-General Manager of the

00:20:37.179 --> 00:20:39.279
American Medical Association, Dr. George Lowe.

00:20:39.279 --> 00:20:40.830
[Carl Ide:] Thank you again Doctor.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:41.830
Dr. Lowe.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:42.830
[Dr. George Lowe:] Mr. Ide.

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:44.990
[Carl Ide:] It's a pleasure to have you coming
here tonight, too.

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:47.000
I believe your home port would be Chicago?

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.440
[Dr. George Lowe:] That's right.

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:51.340
[Carl Ide:] Well, Dr. Lowe, can you explain
a little further about this possible flu epidemic?

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:54.120
[Dr. George Lowe:] Well, in planning for a
possible flu epidemic,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:21:00.179
the medical profession has three responsibilities.

00:21:00.179 --> 00:21:06.559
Keeping the doctors informed on the progress of the disease, spearheading the mobilization

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:11.400
of all medical personnel who will be concerned with the caring for the sick, and informing the public

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:15.000
on what to do once the virus has struck.

00:21:16.119 --> 00:21:22.330
The doctors are being informed about Asian
influenza through our weekly journal,

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:29.590
and through many scientific periodicals published
by the various states and county medical societies.

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:35.779
They are being encouraged to cooperate with
the Public Health Service in detecting

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:39.950
the presence of the virus and its spread from community to community.

00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:46.580
They're being provided with late reports on
the technical aspects of the disease

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:51.769
and with instructions regarding the administration of the vaccine.

00:21:51.769 --> 00:21:57.240
The American Medical Association has asked every local medical society to organize

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:03.299
a committee of those who would be involved in an epidemic so that an agreement can be

00:22:03.299 --> 00:22:10.199
reached in advance on such matters as vaccine
priority, the handling of transients,

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:13.559
hospital admission procedures, and so forth.

00:22:13.559 --> 00:22:20.889
And if an epidemic does strike, the medical
professions will use every means of communication

00:22:20.889 --> 00:22:27.649
possible--pamphlets, newspapers, radio, and television--to tell the general public what to do.

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:32.619
It is important, we feel, that families know
the symptoms of the disease and the basic points

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:36.120
to follow in home care of the flu victim.

00:22:36.129 --> 00:22:38.519
[Carl Ide:] Well, what are those symptoms,
Dr. Lowe?

00:22:38.519 --> 00:22:44.179
[Dr. George Lowe:] Well, the Asian influenza's characterized by a rapid onset high fever,

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:51.760
often as high as 104 degrees, headache, sore throat, aching muscles, and extreme weakness.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:56.379
The recognition of symptoms, of course, is
important early in an epidemic.

00:22:56.379 --> 00:23:00.940
Once the Asian flu takes hold in a community, why, you'll know what it is

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:02.230
without checking the symptoms.

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:06.759
[Carl Ide:] Well, when you do get Asian flu,
Doctor, how do you care for it at home?

00:23:06.759 --> 00:23:11.779
[Dr. George Lowe:] Well ideally, your doctor
should be called, and his instructions followed.

00:23:11.779 --> 00:23:17.879
However, in an epidemic situation, he may
not be able to give your case his complete attention.

00:23:18.119 --> 00:23:21.909
In that event, these are things to remember:

00:23:21.909 --> 00:23:25.620
First, keep away from others.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.220
Second, go to bed.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:30.679
Third, drink a lot of fluids.

00:23:30.679 --> 00:23:32.639
Water and fruit juices.

00:23:32.639 --> 00:23:39.500
And fourth, if your doctor recommends it,
take a fever-reducing agent.

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:44.100
And fifth, call your doctor if any unusual
symptoms develop.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:47.780
[Carl Ide:] What do you mean by unusual symptoms?

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:53.999
[Dr. George Lowe:] Well, primarily those that
would indicate the onset of some lung involvement,

00:23:53.999 --> 00:23:57.210
a heavy cough, rapid breathing, blueness of
the lips.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:58.390
[Carl Ide:] I see.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:00.450
Well thank you very much, Dr. Lowe.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:04.080
And now I'm going to ask Dr. Burney if he
would summarize our discussion.

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:10.320
[Dr. Leroy E. Burney:] This fall and winter
we may see serious outbreaks of influenza

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:16.289
in the United States as a consequence of the epidemics which began last April in the Far East.

00:24:17.779 --> 00:24:23.340
Unlike similar instances in the past however,
we believe that the size and severity

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:28.230
of the epidemics can in this, be minimized
in this country.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:35.389
Thus, it is the opinion of experts that there
is no need for hysteria or panic.

00:24:35.389 --> 00:24:42.159
The influenza is concerned...in the first
place, we are prepared for the invasion.

00:24:42.159 --> 00:24:45.999
And in the second place, we now have the means
through a new vaccine

00:24:45.999 --> 00:24:48.399
to help prevent this influenza.

00:24:48.399 --> 00:24:56.889
Perhaps the story of infection by influenza
virus can best be summarized in this manner:

00:24:56.889 --> 00:25:01.969
There is a pandemic, a worldwide epidemic, of Asian influenza,

00:25:01.969 --> 00:25:06.580
which has attacked most of the nations of the world.

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:11.419
Laboratory tests have shown that it definitely
has reached the United States,

00:25:11.419 --> 00:25:15.190
attacking both military and civilian personnel.

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:21.019
The virus causing this influenza has been
isolated and recognized as a member

00:25:21.019 --> 00:25:25.149
of the familiar type of "A" virus.

00:25:25.149 --> 00:25:31.490
An effective and safe vaccine has been developed
as a preventative, and currently is in production

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:34.789
by six pharmaceutical houses in this country.

00:25:34.789 --> 00:25:42.549
At the present time, Asian influenza can best be classified as a mild, virus disease

00:25:42.549 --> 00:25:48.659
which attacks many people, but has a very
low death rate.

00:25:48.659 --> 00:25:53.710
Experts say that it is probable that an epidemic
will occur in this nation

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:57.639
sometime between late fall and early winter.

00:25:57.639 --> 00:26:05.489
For the first time in history, a nation, our
own United States, is in the fortunate position

00:26:05.489 --> 00:26:10.400
of being ahead of an impending epidemic, and thus has had the time to organize

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:16.779
for an all-out offensive against Asian influenza.

00:26:16.779 --> 00:26:21.190
There is always the possibility of an increase
in virulence of the infection

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:23.230
as the epidemic increases.

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:28.790
However, the new vaccine has been proven effective in control studies conducted

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:37.280
by the military and should be an excellent weapon to combat the disease if the epidemic progresses.

00:26:37.299 --> 00:26:41.899
When the vaccine is made available for public
use, the Public Health Service

00:26:41.899 --> 00:26:47.789
and the American Medical Association hope
that the nation as a whole will participate

00:26:47.789 --> 00:26:54.779
in a voluntary program of vaccination against
the prevalence of this influenza.

00:26:54.779 --> 00:27:00.869
Rest assured that there is no need for alarm,
panic, or hysteria.

00:27:00.869 --> 00:27:05.289
If there is a change in the situation, you
will be informed immediately

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:12.640
through the public education channels of your American Medical Association, United States Public Health Service,

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:16.509
and state and territorial health officers.

00:27:16.509 --> 00:27:22.480
This program, by the way, which is a result
of the combined efforts of organized medicine,

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:29.129
private industry, and education is an excellent
example of the type of public information

00:27:29.129 --> 00:27:31.989
and education we are setting up.

00:27:31.989 --> 00:27:39.289
In closing, I would like to urge you to make
use of the new vaccine when it becomes available,

00:27:39.289 --> 00:27:45.949
knowing meanwhile that you will cooperate
with the medical profession and the public health authorities

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:52.250
if it becomes necessary to set up priorities for those groups responsible for public welfare

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:58.009
and safety and who must continue their
important services if others of us

00:27:58.009 --> 00:28:01.399
are temporarily incapacitated by Asian influenza.

00:28:01.399 --> 00:28:07.519
[Carl Ide:] Well Dr. Burney, I'm sure I speak
for all who have been with us during this program

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:13.020
when I say how much we appreciate all of you gentlemen taking your valuable time to come here

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:18.440
so that we might be better informed on Asian flu and what is being done about it.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:22.540
Participants on tonight's show have been Dr. Leroy E. Burney,

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:26.460
Surgeon General of the United States Public Health Service;

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:32.400
Dr. James A. Crabtree, Associate Dean of the University of Pittsburgh Graduate School of Public Health;

00:28:33.139 --> 00:28:37.639
Dr. Dorland J. Davis, Associate Director in
Charge of Research

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:42.880
of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, United States Public Health Service;

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:48.149
Dr. George F. Lowe, Secretary and General
Manager of the American Medical Association.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:53.700
"The Silent Invader" was written by Tom Coleman.

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:56.899
Produced by Roger Wolfe.

00:28:56.899 --> 00:28:59.620
Directed by Jack Simon.

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:03.639
Script research by Rose Marie Scarpiello and  Kay McDonough.

00:29:03.639 --> 00:29:08.090
"The Silent Invader" has been brought to you as a public service

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.159
by the Westinghouse Broadcasting Company

00:29:10.159 --> 00:29:14.260
in cooperation with the American Medical Association,

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:18.830
the Public Health Service of the U.S. Department of Health, Education and Welfare,

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:21.470
and the University of Pittsburgh Health Center.

